{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>This is the creature that you may eat of all the beasts that are on the earth.</b> If you look at this parashah and at the parashot adjacent to it, you will find them arranged in a great arrangement and with a correct intention according to the order of the four elements from below upward. For you already know that the order of the elements from above downward is the mnemonic ארמ״ע — fire, wind, water, dust — and thus did Solomon arrange them in the Book of Proverbs in the verse (Proverbs 30:4): ״מי עלה שמים וירד / מי אסף רוח בחפניו / מי צרר מים בשמלה / מי הקים כל אפסי ארץ״. But in these parashot you will find them arranged from below upward, and the mnemonic is עמר״א, for he began with the element of dust — this is what is written, ״זאת החיה אשר תאכלו מכל הבהמה אשר על הארץ״ — and in this parashah he explained the beasts and animals, permitted and forbidden, that are of the element of dust. And in the second parashah he mentioned the element of water, and explained the permitted and forbidden fish, and said: ״את זה תאכלו מכל אשר במים״. And in the third parashah he explained the creatures of wind, which are the forbidden and permitted birds, and said: ״ואת אלה תשקצו מן העוף״. And in the fourth parashah he alluded to the element of fire, which is the fourth element and the most subtle of all. And it says: ״וזה לכם הטמא בשרץ השורץ על הארץ החלד והעכבר והצב למינהו״. The צב is two species: one is called ערוד and the second is called salamandra, and this salamandra is alluded to in the word למינהו, which the verse added on its account. And it is a creature born in fire, in an oven that has been heated seven consecutive years day and night, and it grows so much there that if it separates for a moment from the fire it immediately dies — like fish that die when they separate from the water, because the water is their growth and their life; so too this salamandra, the root of its life is the great fire. And on this the rabbis of blessed memory expounded: when Rabbi Akiva would reach this verse, ״והצב למינהו״, he would say: How many are Your works, O YHWH, all of them in wisdom You have made. He created great creatures in the sea and great creatures on dry land — those in the sea, were they to go out to dry land, would immediately die, and those on dry land, were they to enter the sea, would immediately die. He created great creatures in fire and great creatures in the air — those in fire, were they to go out to the air, would immediately die, and those in the air, were they to enter the fire, would immediately die. Hence you say (Psalms 104:24): ״מה רבו מעשיך ה' כלם בחכמה עשית״." "<b>And it appears</b> to me that the reason he used the word אלה for the creatures of wind is that he said ״ואת אלה תשקצו מן העוף״, and also in the passage about the creatures of fire he likewise used the word אלה, saying ״אלה הטמאים לכם״ — because the word אלה is a known spiritual word, from the name Elohim, for the יו״ד and the מ״ם are a plural marker, and the root of the word is אלה. And it comes to allude here that the two elements, which are fire and wind, are subtle elements, and because of their subtlety Scripture attributes them with the name Elohim in many places, saying (Genesis 1:2–3): ״ורוח אלהים מרחפת על פני המים״, (Job 1:16): ״אש אלהים נפלה״. And in the two first parashot, when he mentioned the elements of dust and water, which are heavy, bodily elements, you will not find the word אלה in them at all. And further one may say that he used the language אלה for these two elements in order to allude to idolatry, about which it is written (Exodus 32:4): ״אלה אלהיך ישראל״ — and you already know that there are among the nations those who worship the wind and the fire, because they are light elements and minister to them, so that the abundance of goodness and success may come upon them and their luck may grow stronger, according to their understanding." "<b>By way of the Midrash.</b> ״זאת החיה אשר תאכלו״ — this is what the verse says (Habakkuk 3:6): ״עמד וימודד ארץ / ראה ויתר גוים״. When the Holy One, blessed be He, sought to give the Torah to Israel, He stood and measured the earth and gave the Torah to Israel in the wilderness, in public. Therefore it is written: ״עמד וימודד ארץ״. The blood of the seven nations He permitted to them, as it says (Isaiah 60:12): ״והגוים חרוב יחרבו״. The lives of the seven nations He permitted to them, as it says (Deuteronomy 20:16): ״לא תחיה כל נשמה״. Their property of the seven nations He permitted to them, as it says (ibid. v. 7): ״ואכלת את כל העמים״. Rabbi Shimon ben Yochai says: ״איללן״ — as it says (Numbers 13:16): ״לתור את הארץ״. R. Acha says: He bounded them to Gehenna, as it says (Leviticus 11:21): ״לנתר בהן על הארץ״. Rav Huna of Tzipori said: He loosened them, in the manner of what is written (Job 12:18): ״מוסר מלכים פתח״. Rabbi Tanhum bar Hanilai says: He permitted to them the forbidden things — the detestable things and the creeping things. To what is the matter compared? To a householder who had a threshing floor, and he had with him a donkey and a dog; he loaded the grain upon them. The dog kept throwing off the load, because he was unaccustomed. What did the householder do? He loosened the load from off him. So the Holy One, blessed be He, when He saw the seven nations that there was no moisture in them and they do not fulfill the commandments — to what is the matter compared? To a physician who went to visit two sick men. One was in danger; he said to them: Give him whatever he asks to eat. He entered to the second and said: Give him such-and-such food and do not give him such-and-such food. After some days, the one for whom he had permitted all foods died, and the one for whom he had permitted some and forbidden some recovered. They said to him: What is this? He said to them: This one in whom I saw signs of life — I permitted him this and withheld that from him; and this one in whom I saw signs of death — I permitted him everything. Thus the Holy One, blessed be He, permitted to the seven nations the detestable things and creeping things and all the transgressions, because they are prepared for Gehenna; but Israel, who are for life — He said to them: ״את זה תאכלו ואת זה לא תאכלו״. And therefore He said ״זאת החיה״, which is the language of life, because Israel are attached to life, as it says (Deuteronomy 4:4): ״ואתם הדבקים בה' אלהיכם חיים כלכם היום״."], :book :leviticus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 11, :notes {2 "Method-header restored from the text; the lemma's במדרש carries Rabbeinu Bahya's standard 'By way of the Midrash' tag. The play on לתור/איללן depends on the root תור meaning both 'to scout' and 'to declare permitted'; left hyper-literal as the argument turns on the wordplay. The Job citation ״מוסר מלכים פתח״ has been vocalized to match the verse form."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:25:03.022748643Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}